[2026-10-25 17:52:37] [agentic_pipeline.py (367)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1213777] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is X. You are competing with another player controlling the mark O.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<X({row},{column})>`, where `X` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><X(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to play tic-tac-toe as X. Let me think about the best first move. Since the board is completely empty, I should aim to take the center. Wait, but maybe the center is a good position because it's part of multiple lines. But wait, in the first move, maybe the best is to take the center. Let me check. The center is (1,1). So the action would be X(1,1). But wait, is there any reason to choose another position? Like, maybe the corners? But the center is more strategic. So I think I should go for the center. Let me confirm. Yes, in the first move, taking the center is usually the best strategy. So I'll choose X(1,1).\n</think>\n\n<answer><X(1,1)></answer>user\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\n", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nOkay, so I need to play tic-tac-toe as X. Let me think about the best first move. Since the board is completely empty, I should aim to take the center. Wait, but maybe the center is a good position because it's part of multiple lines. But wait, in the first move, maybe the best is to take the center. Let me check. The center is (1,1). So the action would be X(1,1). But wait, is there any reason to choose another position? Like, maybe the corners? But the center is more strategic. So I think I should go for the center. Let me confirm. Yes, in the first move, taking the center is usually the best strategy. So I'll choose X(1,1).\n</think>\n\n<answer><X(1,1)></answer><|im_end|><|im_start|>user\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\n<|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is O. You are competing with another player controlling the mark X.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<O({row},{column})>`, where `O` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><O(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nCHOSEN ACTION:\n<X(1,1)>\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The game is in Turn 2, and it's my turn as O. The current game state is:\n\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nSo the board has the middle cell (1,1) already taken by X from the previous turn. The legal actions for me are to place an O in any of the remaining cells except (1,1). \n\nFirst, I need to think about the best move to block X from winning or to set up a potential win for myself. Since X just placed in the center, they might be trying to form a line. But since it's my turn, I need to counter that.\n\nLooking at the board, the first row is all empty. The second row has X in the middle. The third row is empty. The possible moves are the corners and the edges except (1,1). \n\nIf I place my O in a corner, maybe that can help block X's possible lines. For example, if X is trying to complete a diagonal, but since X is in the center, they could try to complete a diagonal by getting (0,0) and (2,2). But I need to check if that's possible.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should block X's potential lines. Let's see: X has a mark in the center. If X places in (0,0) next, they could have a diagonal. But since it's my turn, I should prevent that. But wait, the next move is mine, so I can choose where to place O.\n\nAnother approach is to check for possible winning moves. Since I'm O, I need to see if any of my moves can lead to three in a row. But right now, the board is mostly empty except for the center. So maybe I can't win immediately, but I need to block X.\n\nIf I place O in (0,0), that would block X from completing a diagonal. Alternatively, placing O in (2,2) would do the same. But maybe the best move is to take a corner to block X's possible diagonal. Let's say X is trying to get a diagonal, so I should take one of the corners.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should take the center? No, the center is already taken by X. So the options are the corners and the edges. Let me think: if I put O in (0,0), that would block X from completing the diagonal. Similarly, putting O in (2,2) would do the same. But maybe the best move is to take a corner. Let's pick (0,0) as the move. That way, I block X from completing the diagonal. Alternatively, maybe (0,2) or (2,0) or (2,2). But since the first row is empty, maybe taking (0,0) is a good idea. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to take the middle of the top row, but that's (0,1), but that's not blocked. Wait, the legal actions for me are O(0,0), O(0,1), O(0,2), O(1,0), O(1,2), O(2,0), O(2,1), O(2,2). So (0,1) is allowed. But if I place there, that's the middle of the top row. But X is in the center. Maybe that's not as important. \n\nAlternatively, if I place O in (1,0) or (1,2), that would block X from completing a vertical line. For example, if X places in (1,1) and then (1,0), that's a vertical line. But since X is the other player, they might be trying to get a line. But since it's my turn, I need to block. \n\nBut maybe the best move is to take a corner. Let me think again. The current board is:\n\nRow 0: _ _ _\nRow 1: _ X _\nRow 2: _ _ _\n\nSo if I place O in (0,0), then the first row would have O in the first cell. That's a possible start. Alternatively, if I place in (0,2), same thing. But maybe the best is to take a corner to block X's possible diagonal. So I'll choose (0,0) as my move. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the best is to take (1,0) to block X from completing a vertical line. But X is in (1,1), so if X places in (1,0) next, that would be a vertical line. But since it's my turn, I can block that by placing O in (1,