[2026-10-25 19:41:00] [agentic_pipeline.py (367)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1213777] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is X. You are competing with another player controlling the mark O.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<X({row},{column})>`, where `X` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><X(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to play Tic-Tac-Toe as X. The game starts with all cells empty. The first move is crucial because I want to set up a winning position or at least prevent the opponent from winning. Since the board is completely empty, I should consider possible strategies.\n\nIn Tic-Tac-Toe, the best first move is often the center. That's because the center is part of four different lines: the middle row, the middle column, and both diagonals. Controlling the center gives more opportunities to create a line. But wait, maybe I should check if there's any other strategy. Alternatively, corner positions are also good because they are part of three lines each (rows, columns, diagonals). But the center is more strategic for maximum influence.\n\nWait, the first move for X is usually the center. Let me confirm. Yes, in the opening move, taking the center is optimal. So I should choose (1,1). Let me check the legal actions. The legal actions include X(1,1). So the correct move is to place X in the center. That's the best first move to maximize possibilities. So the answer should be <X(1,1)>. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user wants me to choose a corner? But according to standard strategies, center is better. So I'll go with center.\n</think>\n\n<X(1,1)>", "episode_score": -11.0, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to play Tic-Tac-Toe as X. The game starts with all cells empty. The first move is crucial because I want to set up a winning position or at least prevent the opponent from winning. Since the board is completely empty, I should consider possible strategies.\n\nIn Tic-Tac-Toe, the best first move is often the center. That's because the center is part of four different lines: the middle row, the middle column, and both diagonals. Controlling the center gives more opportunities to create a line. But wait, maybe I should check if there's any other stra